            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <p>Using AI to mass-produce ad copy is a standard efficiency play for overseas marketers. But when you launch the campaigns, you notice your creatives look almost identical to competitors, or your multiple variations feel like copies of each other. The problem isn't the AI tool itself—it's how you're using it. Here's a validated checklist to fix homogenization at the source.</p>
<h2>Prompt Optimization: 4 Questions to Ask Before Generating</h2>
<p>Many homogenization issues stem from "prompts that are too broad." Add these constraints to your prompts before generating copy.</p>
<p><strong>Check 1: Have you specified the exact pain point scenario for your target user?</strong></p>
<p>❌ Wrong: "Write an ad copy for a fitness app"<br />
✅ Right: "Write ad copy for a fitness app targeting 25-35 year-old urban professionals who commute to work, have no time for the gym, and quit their gym memberships after visiting fewer than 3 times"</p>
<p><strong>Check 2: Have you explicitly asked AI to avoid common industry phrases?</strong></p>
<p>Add this to your prompt: "Avoid overused phrases like 'revolutionary,' 'groundbreaking,' or 'all-in-one solution'"</p>
<p><strong>Check 3: Have you specified the differentiation angle with keywords?</strong></p>
<p>Price advantage calls for "limited-time discount" or "best value"; emotional resonance requires "finally, no more纠结" or "give yourself a break." Clear angles outperform vague "make it catchy" requests by 10x.</p>
<p><strong>Check 4: Have you requested multiple versions with different styles or angles?</strong></p>
<p>Generate 3-5 variations in one go, explicitly stating "one feature-comparison style, one emotional story style, one data-backed style."</p>
<hr />
<h2>Differentiation Angles: A 5-Point Checklist</h2>
<p>Homogenization happens when everyone uses the same angle. After generating copy, verify against this checklist:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] <strong>User perspective</strong>: Does it use real user dialogue instead of "product description"?</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Usage scenario</strong>: Does it recreate the actual moment the user uses the product?</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Contrast/before-after</strong>: Does it show the user the "change after using"?</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Social proof</strong>: Does it include specific user data or authentic feedback rather than generic "customers love it"?</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Scarcity/urgency</strong>: Does it explain "why now" instead of "available anytime"?</li>
</ul>
<p>Each ad copy should cover at least 2 of these angles to be considered baseline qualified.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Pre-Launch Verification: The Final 5 Minutes</h2>
<p>Before your copy goes live, run through these critical checkpoints:</p>
<p><strong>① Search competitor active creatives</strong><br />
Use tools to view your main competitors' Facebook/TikTok ad libraries. If your copy closely resembles theirs, users will likely scroll past.</p>
<p><strong>② Read it aloud to check if it sounds human</strong><br />
AI copy often has perfect logic but awkward rhythm. If you stumble while reading aloud, adjust sentence structure or shorten phrases.</p>
<p><strong>③ Confirm only 1 core selling point remains</strong><br />
Good ad copy has one protagonist. When two or more selling points appear, you have no selling point at all.</p>
<p><strong>④ Check if CTA is specific</strong><br />
"Start Now," "Click to Claim," "Download &amp; Try" are specific CTAs; "Learn More" or "Discover More" are too vague.</p>
<p><strong>⑤ Preview mobile display</strong><br />
Copy that looks fine on desktop may lose critical information on small mobile screens.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Quick FAQ</h2>
<p><strong>Q: Do I need to completely rewrite AI-generated copy?</strong><br />
A: No. Focus on rewriting the opening 3-second hook and closing CTA. The middle sections can stay as AI output, but ensure consistent tone throughout.</p>
<p><strong>Q: I've identified homogenization issues. What about the old creatives sitting in my account?</strong><br />
A: Pause underperformers (below average by 20%+). Quickly categorize remaining assets using this checklist—keep those with clear differentiation angles, discard the "looks the same" ones.</p>
<p><strong>Q: Without professional copywriters on the team, how do I judge AI copy quality?</strong><br />
A: Two simple tests: Have your least product-savvy colleague review it. If they can't explain "what this product is selling/solving" within 3 seconds, rewrite it.</p>
<hr />
<p>Homogenization isn't an AI problem—it's a "how you use AI" problem. Integrate this checklist into your daily workflow, and you can eliminate at least half of ineffective spend at the creative level.</p>
